WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.830
It's great to welcome to the program today.
Hedrick Smith, who's the executive producer

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.029
of the democracy

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:12.890
belly-up where Hedrick travels the country.
I'm really looking for success stories of

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:17.530
democratic reform, which is, I mean, you know
what I find interesting about this Hedrick

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:21.990
is that there's so much negative going on
and it's not about pretending that everything

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:27.830
is great, but I think it is important to understand
when there are opportunities to make positive

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.269
change. We want to understand both what they
are, but it seems a focus for you also is

00:00:33.269 --> 00:00:38.760
understanding how they can be replicated elsewhere.
Is that a piece of what you're trying to do?

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.039
But you got a bundle of things there. First
place. I didn't actually go looking for successes.

00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:46.899
I was traveling the country. I had written
a book called who stole the American dream

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:52.510
about how we got the inequality in this country
and people will start peppering me at my college

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:56.920
lectures and talks with other groups. How
are we going to fix this as any way doing

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:01.550
anything about it? So I really kind of stumbled
into the store and I began to find that there

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:05.430
were people doing something about student
debt, about minimum wage, and then they were

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:10.270
doing something about gerrymandering and dark
money and voter suppression and things like

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:15.990
that. And I said, wow, this is a story and
it's not being reported. And if you're a good

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:20.060
reporter, finding a story that's good and
that nobody else is reporting are not doing

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.899
much about, that's what you want to go after.

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:27.409
And I was a foreign correspondent for the
New York times for a long time. Moscow, Cairo,

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:31.890
Saigon, Paris. I learned then you can't cover
a country by sitting in the Capitol. And that's

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.770
what our Washington press Corps is doing.
And one of the reasons is the story is really

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:42.110
negative here. It's partisanship, it's gridlock,
it's lobbyist taking over Congress as corporations,

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:47.869
capturing the Congress and people being left
out. But if you get out into Colorado, Washington

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:54.040
or North Carolina or Florida or South Dakota
of all places, Connecticut, Massachusetts,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.950
people are on the move and they're fixing
things. And I just thought that's a tremendous

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:58.950
story.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:04.460
A lot of the stories you, stories you tell
relate to money and politics in some way,

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:09.060
shape or form. Sometimes it's very direct.
It's literally about money. Sometimes it's

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:15.760
more indirectly related to the influence that
one can buy or wield if they have access to

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:21.960
money, sometimes hidden money in different
ways. Is this the number one theme in a sense

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:27.600
that what we need to be looking at at as a
starting point is the way that money flows

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.480
into politics or is there some other theme
that you would assign to the stories that

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.480
you found?

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.030
Well, I, the most important theme is that
people power works. There's a, there's an

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:43.920
enormous dissatisfaction and anger and disenchantment
among the American people today with a political

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.420
system, a sense that Washington and politicians
don't listen to people like me. Uh, that the

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:54.240
big money is captured Congress and the government
is working for special interests. Uh, and

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.660
along with that, there's a widespread feeling
of powerlessness, of hopelessness. There's

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:02.621
nothing we can do about it. They've got all
the money. They've got a lock on power. We

00:03:02.621 --> 00:03:07.010
can't get them out of office. They won't listen
to us. Uh, and uh, a friend of mine, Ernie

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:12.280
Cortez, who's an organizer down in Texas in
the Southwest said to me at one point, you

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:18.750
know, people say power corrupts. Absolute
power corrupts. Absolutely. He said, but powerlessness.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:24.740
Powerlessness also corrupts. It corrupts democracy
at the core. But what astonished me wasn't

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:31.850
there is, I don't know, eight to 8%, 10% 15%
20% of people out there who say, dammit, I'm

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:32.850
going to do something about it.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.070
We got to take this on ourselves. That's the
main theme. The main theme is that people

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:42.940
power does work. I mean, last year 20 the
last election, 2018 was the best year for

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.710
political reform in this country. In half
a century, five States adopted gerrymander

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:51.490
reform, three adopted elect reform, seven
or eight States made it easier to vote. Four

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:56.680
or five big cities adopted public funding
of campaigns, a Maine adopted rank order voting

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:02.710
there. There are now a 13 States sign on to
change the electoral college. So reform is

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:06.870
actually springing up all over the place.
So that's the main thing. Yes, money is a

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:12.650
big deal, but gerrymandering is another big
deal. Any way people can get a lock on power

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:18.630
either by manipulating election maps or by
using dark money and big money. That's dangerous

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.430
to democracy and people are reacting and that's
great.

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:25.040
Yeah. There's been some successes when it
comes to Jerry Mandarin. We saw what happened

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.340
in North Carolina. We saw what happened in
Florida. What I'm interested in hearing from

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:37.280
you about this is gerrymandering is extraordinarily
important and impactful, but for a lot of

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:41.500
people who are just through no fault of their
own, they're just busy living their lives.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.161
They're trying to put food on the table, they're
going to work and doing these things. It's

00:04:45.161 --> 00:04:51.440
not an issue that as many people are aware
of as maybe should be. What was it that you

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:58.410
believe took place in the places where they've
had successes that created the activism and

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:02.570
the awareness of this as an issue? Like what's
the difference maker with regard to getting

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:08.320
people engaged and saying, this is an issue
we can activate around, let's do it.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.030
Speaking up and doing some of the about it
in Michigan, a young woman named Katie Fe,

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:21.660
28 years old posted a post on Facebook. This
is back in 2017 posted on Facebook that gerrymandering

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:27.620
was going on in Michigan that the state Republicans
in the state legislature had tilted the scales.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:32.420
And so instead of there being a sort of a
50 50 split in the state's legislature and

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:37.220
in the States delegation to Congress, which
is about what the popular vote was, there

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.970
was a real tilt in favor of the Republicans.
So she writes this on Facebook. Within a week

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:47.520
she got 5,000 responses. She set up a group
called voters, not politicians, meaning voters

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:53.260
should draw the lines, voters should decide,
not politicians drawing the map. She understood

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:57.540
that Jerry Manning was stacking the deck and
therefore it was affecting everything else.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:02.081
It was affecting how they voted on minimum
wage, on student debt, on immigration, on

00:06:02.081 --> 00:06:03.660
gun control, everything else.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:08.230
And what happens is usually people focus on
their issue and that's understandable. There

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.660
are certain issues that really grabbed people,
aggravate them, they want to do some about

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:17.220
it. But what Katie Fe and others have understood
is that fixing gerrymandering, fixing money

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:22.490
and politics, fixing voters rights is the
gateway to the issues that you want to get

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:27.280
to. If the system is tilted and stacked in
favor of special interests, then no matter

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.600
how how loud you scream, no matter how much
you organized, you can't change it. And I

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.080
think that's a real awareness. I don't know
what it is, why they responded. The answer

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.510
was a whole lot of people in Michigan understood
what Katie Faiz said and when she finally

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.820
lit the match, it struck a spark and there
it went. But it's interesting. It wasn't just

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:48.690
Michigan. I mean it happened in Missouri.
It happened in Colorado.

00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:54.100
It happened in Ohio. You know it happened
in Utah and North Dakota. There's a bunch

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:59.300
of 70 year old women who call themselves the
listen to this, the bad ass grandmas of normal.

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:04.020
They're 70 years old. There are half a dozen
of them. They said, we have a corrupt political

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:08.380
system. We got foreign money coming in. We
got lobbyist with too much power. We're not

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:13.110
limiting contributions. They literally passed
a referendum and North Dakota pushed by the

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:17.870
grant passed grandmas. Now, I can't tell you
why it took off. I think the answer is there's

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:23.290
so much tender out there, there's so much
anger, there's so much frustration with an

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:29.820
unfair political system that if the person
lights the right match, it takes off. And

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:34.801
the answer is it's taken off in state after
state. So there's no special magic. Uh, I

00:07:34.801 --> 00:07:40.540
think the key is people hanging in there and
people getting over that sense that we're

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:41.960
powerless, we can't do it.

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:46.810
And I think that's the reason why our movie,
the democracy rebellion is really important

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:52.750
because it shows people that in States is
different. As North Carolina and South Dakota,

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:58.760
California in Florida, Connecticut and Washington,
people power has worked. It's not just a dream.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:03.560
It's not just, uh, what you learned in high
school. It's not just civics. It's actually

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:07.720
happening. And I think when people see it,
they'll say, and the guy says at the end of

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:12.700
the documentary, it's a wonderful line of
guy named Jorge Misoula, who's a Latino organizer

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:17.060
in Miami. He says, if we can do it in Florida,
you can do it in your state. I think that's

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:18.060
the message.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:23.570
Uh, is there a, uh, or, let me, let me frame
it up in a different way. There are different,

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:29.350
uh, mechanisms through which we can achieve
change. So sometimes it's a voting for someone

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:34.069
different to represent us in a particular,
you know, state Senate, house of representatives,

00:08:34.069 --> 00:08:38.349
whatever. There are ballot initiatives that
sometimes have been successful at achieving

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:44.259
change. There are legal appeals, so filing
lawsuits and appealing those up to a high

00:08:44.259 --> 00:08:50.589
enough level at which, uh, a change can be
adjudicated. Are different mechanisms more

00:08:50.589 --> 00:08:56.160
or less appropriate for different goals or
is there one mechanism that you believe is

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:57.439
most effective?

00:08:57.439 --> 00:09:01.579
No, the more arrows you have in your quiver,
they're better off you are the people, the

00:09:01.579 --> 00:09:05.889
people, for example, in North Carolina who
were working in the moral Monday movement

00:09:05.889 --> 00:09:12.209
in North Carolina, which was working against
a photo ID law that one of the courts said

00:09:12.209 --> 00:09:18.959
was targeted with surgical precision to affect
the voting of African American voters. Okay.

00:09:18.959 --> 00:09:23.470
So they went to court and they filed suit.
And eventually they won that court decision.

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:28.220
But they also had demonstrations outside the
state. Capitol moral Monday, they started

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:33.620
out with a a dozen or two dozen people. They
wound up at one point with 80,000 people on

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:39.629
one Monday. So they use mass demonstrations
both to show the legislators, the law makers

00:09:39.629 --> 00:09:44.430
that they had to respond to public opinion,
but also to mobilize people. And actually

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:48.769
that's effective. Uh, the way elections is
starting to go in North Carolina, it mobilized

00:09:48.769 --> 00:09:52.790
people not just for that issue, but it gave
people the idea that if they came out, they

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:57.309
could influence the state government. They
actually want a Conrad, I mean a governor's

00:09:57.309 --> 00:10:01.389
election in North Carolina. So that, I mean,
the politics of North Ghana have begun to

00:10:01.389 --> 00:10:06.800
change as a result of the protest movement.
So now there's no, there's no silver bullet.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.220
There's no one answer. And matter of fact,
the more techniques that are used, the better

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:16.560
chance of success, a protest movement or a
democracy rebellion has a then if it just

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:18.029
tries one thing,

00:10:18.029 --> 00:10:23.850
there are people who will hear our conversation
and certainly with, we'll agree with the outcomes

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:30.499
of many of the stories that are told in the
film, but they will no doubt, still say, listen

00:10:30.499 --> 00:10:35.809
at the national level. When it comes to what
we see happening in the white house, we feel

00:10:35.809 --> 00:10:41.360
helpless. We see that there are checks and
balances, but they're being ignored. We see

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:46.839
that there has been an impeachment, which
even if it were to go, uh, to the Senate,

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:53.579
would, um, ultimately, uh, be, uh, an acquittal
when it comes to how the Senate would vote

00:10:53.579 --> 00:10:58.930
and they feel powerless. What do you say to
people who are concerned about at the national

00:10:58.930 --> 00:11:04.410
level, what they've felt has been an inability
despite what Donald Trump has done, no matter

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:08.240
what it is that has taken place, that they
feel as though it's just a matter of either

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.519
we vote him out in November or we don't. And
otherwise we feel like we don't have that

00:11:11.519 --> 00:11:12.779
much power to affect change.

00:11:12.779 --> 00:11:17.360
Well, if you're going to go mountain climbing,
you don't start up by, by going after Everest,

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.660
you start with a Hill and your own neighborhood
and you climb it, and then you learn how to

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:24.990
climb a bigger Hill. Uh, I mean, I think the
answer is if you're looking at what's going

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:29.260
on in the Senate, when you're looking at what's
going on in the Congress, those are directly

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:35.380
affected by the reforms that are taking place
at the state level. Now, if Donald Trump is

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:40.220
defeated in November, the reform movements
I'm talking about will have had something

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:45.619
to do with mobilizing people and giving them
a sense of power. Uh, it, you know, politics

00:11:45.619 --> 00:11:49.670
is not a light switch. You can't throw a light
switch and say, Oh, well, if I go out and

00:11:49.670 --> 00:11:54.500
demonstrate today, I get whatever it is I
want politically. Tomorrow. You've got to

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:55.500
build for it.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:59.279
You got to work for it. I mean, I mean, Trump's
election, whether or not people like it or

00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:04.829
not, was a Mark of enormous dissatisfaction
by at least 60 million American people, not

00:12:04.829 --> 00:12:09.089
as many Hillary Clinton, God, but they were
very unhappy with the party. On the other

00:12:09.089 --> 00:12:12.559
side. You've got to acknowledge that. Part
of the difficulty is we get so locked into

00:12:12.559 --> 00:12:16.260
our point of view that we think, well, we're
right and the other side is totally wrong

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:19.810
and there's no justification there. The answer
is you've got to go out and persuade people.

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:23.529
Well, one way you persuade people is you get
involved in a movement. One of the things

00:12:23.529 --> 00:12:29.040
that, that, that is data that has really struck
me about this movement is how trans partisan

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.970
it is. When I go into a Colorado or I go into
a Connecticut or I go into Florida, I find

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:37.139
Republicans, independents, and Democrats all
working on it.

00:12:37.139 --> 00:12:41.709
If I'm in Washington, where I sit and where
I live and where I've done my political reporting

00:12:41.709 --> 00:12:48.040
most of my life, I see it just absolute cleavage.
I see a grand Canyon. There used to be an

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:53.029
overlap in the parties. Politicians, I mean
the political scientists used to do charts

00:12:53.029 --> 00:12:57.459
and they actually plotted the votes of the
Democrats in the house and the Republicans

00:12:57.459 --> 00:13:01.610
in the house. And they overlapped. And today
there's a Gulf between them. They actually

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:06.029
chart them the maps and they look different.
That's not the case out in the country. So

00:13:06.029 --> 00:13:09.939
the place where we're going to get the country
back is out there. And by the way, that's

00:13:09.939 --> 00:13:14.389
American history. I mean the civil rights
movement didn't start in Washington. They

00:13:14.389 --> 00:13:20.579
passed, uh, the civil rights act of 1964 and
the voting rights act of 1965 after years

00:13:20.579 --> 00:13:22.699
of protest at the grassroots.

00:13:22.699 --> 00:13:25.800
So if you were sitting at the grassroots,
if you were involved in the Montgomery bus

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:30.889
boycott in 1955, you had to wait six, seven,
eight, nine years before it happened. But

00:13:30.889 --> 00:13:35.889
it did happen and you had an impact on it.
I was in Birmingham, I was in Albany, Georgia.

00:13:35.889 --> 00:13:40.999
I was in Nashville, Tennessee. I saw the thing
built. What I'm seeing as a reporter today

00:13:40.999 --> 00:13:45.810
is the same kind of building. It will yield
results. It is already yielding results. At

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:51.040
the state level, the democracy rebellion is
leading, is getting those results. It will

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:55.690
lead results to the national level. We saw
it with a progressive era a century ago. Women

00:13:55.690 --> 00:13:59.959
got the right to vote. We got direct election,
the senators, we've had this kind of period

00:13:59.959 --> 00:14:06.819
in American history before we shot it. It's
smart to take heart. It's smart to take inspiration

00:14:06.819 --> 00:14:09.619
and not to do what Ernie Cortez said.

00:14:09.619 --> 00:14:14.760
Feel powerless because you can't knock the
guy off in the way you want at the moment.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:20.319
I understand that frustration. Uh, but uh,
politics takes time. I mean, it's a little

00:14:20.319 --> 00:14:25.389
bit like watching grass growing. I mean, if
you watch a long, you can't see any change,

00:14:25.389 --> 00:14:28.950
but if you were there in the spring and it
was bare ground and you're there in the fall

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:33.279
and there's a lawn, you understand something
was happening. That's what I think is going

00:14:33.279 --> 00:14:39.439
on in America today. The grass is growing.
Uh, the, the land is fro, the people are moving.

00:14:39.439 --> 00:14:44.499
Uh, it works. It's amazing. I mean, I have
to tell you, as a reporter who's reported

00:14:44.499 --> 00:14:49.149
for five decades has been all over the world.
I've reported from 40 countries. Uh, so I'm

00:14:49.149 --> 00:14:54.199
amazed at what I'm seeing and I as a citizen,
I'm really encouraged.

00:14:54.199 --> 00:14:57.790
We've been speaking about the film, the democracy
rebellion with the [inaudible]

00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:02.069
film's executive producer, Hedrick Smith Hedrick.
I really appreciate your time today and I

00:15:02.069 --> 00:15:06.959
encourage the audience to absolutely check
out the film. Be great to have people watch

00:15:06.959 --> 00:15:08.269
it. I think you'll take heart. It'll inspire
you.

